  "title": "Length of surreal product",
  "statement": {
    "representation": "markdown-latex",
    "text": "Conjecture Every surreal number has a unique sign expansion, i.e. function $f: o\\rightarrow \\{-, +\\}$, where $o$ is some ordinal. This $o$ is the length of given sign expansion and also the birthday of the corresponding surreal number. Let us denote this length of $s$ as $\\ell(s)$.\n\nIt is easy to prove that\n\n$$\\ell(s+t) \\leq \\ell(s)+\\ell(t)$$\n\nWhat about\n\n$$\\ell(s\\times t) \\leq \\ell(s)\\times\\ell(t)$$?",
